LIPPS should not describe either course as high-stakes-ready until all of the following are complete:

second subject-matter-expert review;
explicit course assessment blueprint;
authentic assignments and rubrics;
mastery-bank rewrite;
accessibility review;
native Quarto render and browser/mobile testing;
Moodle import test on the actual hosted instance;
student pilot;
item analysis after pilot;
revision based on completion, difficulty, discrimination and response-time data; and
local curriculum/credit-hour mapping by the adopting institution.

By the end of the course, learners should be able to

frame answerable epidemiological questions;
calculate and interpret common measures of disease frequency;
select and interpret measures of association and effect;
distinguish major observational and experimental study designs;
identify common sources of bias, confounding and effect modification;
interpret screening and diagnostic-test performance in context;
describe the logic of surveillance and outbreak investigation;
distinguish association from defensible causal interpretation; and
critically appraise epidemiological claims in reports, papers and programme data.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 5, you should be able to:

define a cross-sectional study from the timing of exposure and outcome measurement;
explain why cross-sectional studies naturally estimate prevalence rather than incidence;
distinguish target population, study population, sampling frame, sampling unit and observation unit;
identify when a census or sample is appropriate;
recognise how selection and non-response can alter prevalence estimates;
identify when survey weights, clustering and stratification affect analysis;
distinguish a prevalence ratio, prevalence difference and odds ratio as alternative ways to compare cross-sectional outcomes;
explain the temporality problem when exposure and outcome are measured at the same time;
recognise how disease duration can influence observed prevalence;
write conclusions that match what a cross-sectional design can and cannot establish.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 2, you should be able to:

distinguish a count, ratio, proportion and rate;
identify the appropriate numerator, denominator, population at risk and time period for a frequency measure;
calculate and interpret incidence proportion (risk);
calculate and interpret attack rates and secondary attack rates in outbreak settings;
calculate and interpret incidence rates using person-time;
calculate and distinguish point and period prevalence;
explain conceptually why prevalence is influenced by both incidence and duration;
calculate and distinguish major mortality measures including crude, cause-specific and stratum-specific mortality;
distinguish case fatality and proportionate mortality from population mortality rates;
interpret selected maternal, neonatal, infant and natality measures; and
recognise when population structure makes a crude comparison potentially misleading.

DEMONSTRATE · Week 2 Mastery Quiz
The Week 2 Moodle bank is built around measure selection, denominator recognition, compact calculations and interpretation.
Pilot specification

15 questions sampled from a 50-item Week 2 bank;
12 minutes initially because this week includes short calculations;
one question at a time;
question and answer order randomised where appropriate;
calculated items varied across equivalent numerical scenarios;
one graded attempt in Mastery mode;
Practice mode gives explanatory feedback; and
institutional accommodations can extend the time without changing the learning objective.


AI-resilient by design
The assessment is not protected by disabling copy and paste. It is protected by preparation: a learner who understands the measure can identify the denominator and calculate the answer faster than someone repeatedly outsourcing each question.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 7, you should be able to:

recognise a cohort study from exposure-based grouping and subsequent outcome ascertainment;
distinguish prospective and retrospective cohort studies;
explain why cohort studies can estimate incidence directly;
calculate and interpret risk ratios and risk differences in cohort data;
explain when person-time incidence rates are needed;
describe censoring and unequal follow-up conceptually;
recognise how loss to follow-up can reduce precision or introduce selection bias;
choose an appropriate unexposed or comparison cohort;
distinguish fixed from changing exposures conceptually;
identify major strengths and limitations of cohort designs.

LEARN 10 · Strengths and limitations
Strengths

preserves temporal ordering more clearly than a typical cross-sectional design;
directly measures incidence;
can evaluate multiple outcomes from one exposure;
useful for rare exposures;
allows study of natural history and change over time.

Limitations

inefficient for very rare outcomes unless the cohort is huge;
prospective studies can be slow and expensive;
loss to follow-up can threaten validity;
exposure may change;
observational cohorts remain vulnerable to confounding.

A long follow-up does not automatically mean a strong study. The quality of measurement and comparison remains decisive.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 11, you should be able to:

define public-health surveillance and distinguish it from one-off research and direct control activity;
state how surveillance objectives determine data needs and system design;
distinguish passive, active, sentinel and syndromic surveillance approaches;
analyse routine surveillance by time, place and person;
explain key surveillance attributes including timeliness, sensitivity, representativeness, quality and predictive value positive;
distinguish a cluster, outbreak, epidemic and expected background occurrence;
outline the conceptual steps of an outbreak investigation without treating them as a rigid sequence;
develop a workable case definition and case-finding strategy;
organise field data using a line list and descriptive epidemiology;
choose between retrospective cohort and case-control approaches in common outbreak settings;
reconsider hypotheses when analytic results do not fit the descriptive evidence;
explain why control measures may sometimes begin before every uncertainty is resolved;
communicate findings and recommendations in a way that supports action.

ORIENT · A surveillance signal is not yet an outbreak
A district dashboard shows 18 suspected meningitis cases this week compared with 4–7 in recent weeks.
That signal should trigger attention, but it does not yet tell you:

whether all 18 reports meet the same case definition;
whether duplicates are present;
whether laboratory or reporting practices changed;
whether the population denominator changed;
whether cases are linked by person, place or time; or
whether the increase exceeds an appropriate expected baseline.

Field epidemiology begins by converting signal → verified problem → description → hypothesis → evidence → action.

LEARN 5 · Surveillance attributes are trade-offs
A well-conducted surveillance system is judged against its purpose.
Important attributes discussed in the attached CDC material include:

acceptability — willingness to participate;
flexibility — ability to adapt;
quality — completeness and validity of data;
representativeness — how well observed patterns reflect the population;
sensitivity — ability to detect true cases or events;
predictive value positive — proportion of detected signals/cases that are true;
simplicity — ease of operation;
stability — reliability and availability;
timeliness — information arriving soon enough for action; and
validity — whether the system measures what it intends to measure.

Improving one attribute can worsen another. Higher sensitivity, for example, may increase false alarms and reduce predictive value positive.

LEARN 7 · Outbreak investigation is systematic but not rigid
The attached CDC lesson presents outbreak investigation steps as a conceptual plan, not a sequence that must always be executed one-by-one in exactly the same order.
A practical sequence is:

prepare for field work;
establish whether an outbreak exists;
verify the diagnosis;
develop a workable case definition;
find and record cases systematically;
describe cases by time, place and person;
develop hypotheses;
evaluate hypotheses using epidemiology, laboratory and environmental evidence;
refine hypotheses when needed;
implement and evaluate control measures;
communicate findings.

Several steps often occur simultaneously.

LEARN 9 · Case finding and the line list
After defining a case, investigators actively identify additional cases through facilities, laboratories, communities, schools, workplaces, contacts or media alerts as appropriate.
A line list should allow investigators to:

detect duplicates;
update case status;
analyse onset over time;
map relevant locations;
describe affected populations;
track laboratory results and outcomes; and
identify missing information.

Good field epidemiology combines speed with disciplined data structure.

LEARN 12 · When the first hypothesis fails
Analytic epidemiology can be unrevealing because:

the leading hypothesis was wrong;
exposure was measured poorly;
the relevant exposure was not included;
controls were inappropriate;
the outbreak contains multiple mechanisms; or
sample size is limited.

The attached text explicitly recommends revisiting descriptive epidemiology, outliers and interviews when initial analyses fail.
Failure to confirm a hypothesis is not failure of the investigation. It is information that should change the next question.

LEARN 14 · Communication closes the loop
Surveillance and outbreak findings must reach those who can act.
Communication may include:

rapid operational updates;
technical briefs;
feedback to reporting facilities;
community communication;
formal scientific reports; and
recommendations to decision-makers.

The attached CDC material emphasises scientific objectivity, defensible conclusions and written reports that document methods, results, discussion and recommendations.
A field investigation is incomplete if the evidence never reaches the people responsible for control.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 4, you should be able to:

explain what descriptive epidemiology contributes before analytic testing;
distinguish the health event being counted from the characteristics used to describe it;
construct the logic of a useful line list and recognise why data quality matters before plotting;
select an appropriate time scale and distinguish secular, seasonal and outbreak-time patterns;
interpret the basic purpose and limitations of an epidemic curve;
describe disease by place using relevant geographic units, rates and spot maps;
choose person characteristics and category widths that preserve important patterns;
distinguish a high count from a high rate;
use descriptive patterns to identify populations at risk and generate hypotheses;
recognise outliers and apparent increases that may reflect reporting, denominator or measurement changes rather than true disease change.

LEARN 5 · Outliers can be clues rather than rubbish
An observation far from the main pattern may represent:

a data-entry or recall error;
an unrelated illness;
an unusually long or short incubation period;
a secondary case;
exposure at a different time or place; or
the clue that links apparently separate groups.

The correct response is not automatically to delete it. Verify it, understand it, and document any exclusion.
This is a useful LIPPS habit:

Investigate surprising observations before deciding they are errors.

LEARN 10 · Observed versus expected
A cluster is not automatically an outbreak. Investigators must consider whether occurrence exceeds what is expected for that population and period.
The expected level may come from:

prior weeks, months or years;
a comparable season;
surveillance records;
another population with similar characteristics; or
a deliberately constructed baseline.

An apparent increase may instead reflect population growth, improved diagnostics, duplicate reporting, a changed case definition, greater public attention or a more diligent reporting facility.

LIPPS question: What changed in the disease—and what changed in the system that observes the disease?

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 9, you should be able to:

define bias as systematic error rather than mere random variation;
distinguish selection bias from information bias;
recognise non-differential and differential misclassification conceptually;
define confounding and identify the conditions that make a variable a plausible confounder;
distinguish confounders from mediators and colliders at an introductory level;
explain why indiscriminate statistical adjustment can create rather than remove bias;
distinguish confounding from effect modification;
interpret stratum-specific estimates when effects differ across groups;
recognise that interaction can be defined on different statistical scales;
use design and subject-matter knowledge, not p-values alone, to decide what to adjust for.

LEARN 2 · Selection bias
Selection bias arises when the way people enter, remain in, or are analysed in a study creates a systematic difference related to both exposure and outcome.
Examples:

hospital controls whose illnesses are related to the exposure;
differential loss to follow-up in a cohort;
participation in a survey related to health status and exposure;
excluding participants because of post-exposure events.

Selection bias is often created before analysis. Statistical adjustment cannot always reconstruct the population that should have been observed.

LEARN 3 · Information bias
Information bias arises from systematic differences in how exposure, outcome or other variables are measured.
Examples include:

cases recalling past exposure more intensely than controls;
interviewers probing one group more thoroughly;
an outcome assessor knowing treatment assignment;
different laboratory methods across study sites;
self-reported behaviours affected by social desirability.

Measurement error can be differential when its pattern differs according to another relevant variable, or non-differential when the same classification process applies across groups.

LIPPS extension The attached CDC glossary defines information bias; the differential/non-differential distinction is added here because it is central to applied interpretation.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish primary, secondary and influential audiences;
conduct a basic audience analysis using needs, beliefs, norms, barriers, channels and decision context;
explain segmentation and its benefits and risks;
apply health-literacy and plain-language principles;
distinguish literacy from intelligence and health literacy from general schooling;
communicate numbers and risk more clearly;
adapt communication across language and cultural contexts without stereotyping;
identify accessibility and digital-exclusion barriers;
use formative research and community listening to test assumptions.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

explain why theory can improve communication planning;
distinguish determinants of behaviour from messages intended to influence them;
apply key constructs from the Health Belief Model, Theory of Planned Behaviour and Social Cognitive Theory;
use the social-ecological perspective to distinguish individual, interpersonal, community and structural influences;
apply COM-B as a practical diagnostic framework for capability, opportunity and motivation;
explain diffusion of innovations concepts including relative advantage, compatibility, complexity, trialability and observability;
choose a theory because it fits the problem rather than because it is familiar;
translate theoretical constructs into measurable communication objectives.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish health, behavioural and communication problems;
write specific measurable communication objectives;
develop a communication theory of change;
align audience, mechanism, message, messenger and channel;
distinguish inputs, activities, outputs, reach, intermediate outcomes, behaviour and health outcomes;
select process and outcome indicators;
explain implementation fidelity and dose/exposure;
choose feasible evaluation designs and recognise attribution limits;
combine quantitative and qualitative evaluation evidence;
assess equity, cost and unintended effects.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

describe strengths and limitations of major digital communication formats;
select digital platforms according to audience behaviour and objective;
distinguish impressions, reach, engagement, click-through and conversion;
explain algorithmic amplification and attention dynamics;
design social-listening and moderation approaches;
identify privacy, consent and confidentiality risks;
apply mobile-first and low-bandwidth design principles;
recognise digital exclusion and inequity;
use digital analytics without overstating impact;
identify appropriate roles and risks of automated and AI-assisted communication.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

translate a communication objective into a message strategy;
distinguish core message, supporting evidence and call to action;
apply clarity, relevance, credibility and actionability criteria;
use framing carefully without distorting evidence;
write plain-language health messages;
identify visual and numerical communication risks;
design a message pre-test;
distinguish comprehension, relevance, acceptability, credibility and intended action;
revise messages using evidence rather than preference;
recognise when adaptation is needed instead of direct translation.

For universities
LIPPS courses are designed so they can be used as supplementary digital courseware rather than replacing the lecturer or the institution’s curriculum.
A university-facing implementation can provide:

a 12-week structured course;
weekly objective quizzes and reusable question banks;
instructor access to grades and completion;
cohort-level performance reporting;
institution-defined assessment timing and accommodations;
links from course lessons into the public LIPPS Methods library and Projects;
Moodle/LMS integration options as the institutional product develops.

Talk to LIPPS Studio about institutional use →

The course now has a coherent progression:
Think epidemiologically → measure frequency → compare groups → describe patterns → understand observational designs → understand intervention designs → recognise threats to validity → evaluate tests → conduct surveillance/outbreak reasoning → critically interpret evidence.
Each new week contains:

explicit learning outcomes;
an orienting public-health problem;
explanatory teaching sections;
applied examples or calculations;
a larger integrated problem;
practice questions;
a timed mastery specification;
source/adaptation notes; and
forward/back course navigation.

The current 680 items are pilot teaching items, not a psychometrically validated examination bank. Before formal high-stakes use:

have a second epidemiology subject-matter expert review all items;
pilot items with students;
examine item difficulty, discrimination and distractor performance;
examine response times and non-response near timeout;
rewrite ambiguous or overly cueable distractors;
check calculation tolerances and Moodle import behaviour;
review accessibility with keyboard and screen-reader workflows;
check local curriculum alignment and assessment regulations; and
run native quarto render once Quarto is installed in the deployment environment.

Week 2 focuses on measuring disease frequency and population health. It is intentionally separated from Week 3, which will handle measures of association and effect.



