Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

define emergency risk communication and distinguish it from routine health promotion;
explain why timeliness and trust are operational assets during crises;
communicate what is known, unknown and being done to reduce uncertainty;
identify factors influencing risk perception;
develop concise action-oriented emergency messages;
coordinate messengers and information across response partners;
integrate community engagement and feedback into emergency communication;
manage corrections and changing guidance without pretending certainty;
design a basic communication response across preparedness, initial response and recovery phases.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

define health communication and distinguish it from information dissemination, publicity and health education alone;
describe communication as a transactional process involving message, channel, audience, context, feedback and noise;
distinguish communication objectives from broader programme and health outcomes;
identify major levels of health communication: interpersonal, organisational, community, mass-media and digital;
explain why credibility, accessibility, relevance, timeliness, understandability and actionability matter;
recognise that audiences interpret messages through prior beliefs, trust, language, experience and social context;
identify common reasons technically accurate health messages fail;
apply a basic communication diagnosis before proposing a message or channel.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

describe interpersonal communication as a two-way clinical and public-health process;
use open questions, reflective listening and summarising appropriately;
apply teach-back without shaming the patient;
distinguish informing, counselling, persuasion and shared decision-making;
explain how empathy, respect and non-verbal behaviour influence trust;
communicate risk and uncertainty in understandable terms;
work more safely across language and interpreter needs;
recognise confidentiality, stigma and power problems in clinical communication;
identify when motivational interviewing techniques may support behaviour change without coercion.

Every future mastery or institutional question should, where possible:

begin with a plausible public-health decision or communication situation;
require application, analysis or evaluation rather than term recognition alone;
use distractors that reflect realistic reasoning errors;
have one defensibly best answer;
give explanatory feedback that teaches the reasoning, not merely labels the answer;
avoid testing trivia that could be answered without understanding the communication problem;
distinguish communication failure from service, policy or access failure; and
avoid overclaiming causation from reach, recall or simple before/after observations.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish misinformation, disinformation, rumours and infodemics;
explain why information voids and uncertainty create fertile conditions for harmful narratives;
use social listening to identify questions and emerging narratives;
prioritise misinformation responses according to reach, harm, vulnerability and actionability;
choose when to correct publicly and when not to amplify a low-reach claim;
write corrections that lead with useful facts and concrete actions;
explain prebunking and resilience-building approaches;
integrate community engagement with digital responses;
measure infodemic-response outputs and outcomes;
recognise ethical risks in monitoring information environments.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish interpersonal, community, mass-media and digital channels;
choose channels according to communication objective rather than fashion;
explain reach, frequency, exposure and engagement;
assess channel credibility, accessibility and cost;
distinguish owned, earned, paid and shared media;
design an integrated channel mix;
select and prepare credible messengers and spokespersons;
recognise channel-specific risks including exclusion and message distortion;
develop a basic media plan and monitoring framework.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

apply autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice and truthfulness to communication decisions;
distinguish persuasion from manipulation and coercion;
identify stigma, fear and dignity risks in campaign design;
assess privacy and data-protection risks in digital communication;
design communication for equity and inclusion;
recognise representation and power issues in imagery and storytelling;
identify risks of AI-generated and personalised health communication;
specify human oversight and verification requirements for AI use;
evaluate unintended effects and conflicts of interest;
apply the LIPPS SIGNAL framework to a complete communication problem.

Learning outcomes
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish information, consultation, involvement, collaboration and shared leadership;
explain why community engagement is different from mobilisation alone;
map stakeholders by influence, interest, trust and role;
identify power and representation problems in community participation;
design feedback and accountability loops;
use community listening to identify questions, rumours and barriers;
explain the relationship between community engagement and trust;
apply RCCE principles in health emergencies and humanitarian settings;
recognise safeguarding, inclusion and do-no-harm considerations.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 1, you should be able to:

define epidemiology and explain the meaning of distribution, determinants, specified populations, and application;
distinguish the clinical view of an individual patient from the epidemiological view of a population;
organise a public-health problem using what, who, where, when, and why/how;
distinguish frequency from pattern, and explain why a denominator matters;
distinguish introductory descriptive from analytic epidemiological reasoning;
explain why valid comparison groups are central to analytic epidemiology;
identify major historical contributions of Hippocrates, John Graunt, William Farr, and John Snow;
describe major uses and core functions of epidemiology in public-health practice;
explain the simple epidemiological sequence define → count → divide → compare → interpret → act; and
convert a vague health concern into a focused epidemiological question.

DEMONSTRATE · Week 1 Mastery Quiz
The Moodle mastery quiz is designed as a short, objective, scenario-based assessment.
Pilot specification

15 questions sampled from a 40-item Week 1 bank;
10 minutes initially, then calibrated using real student response-time data;
one question at a time;
question order and answer positions randomised where appropriate;
one graded attempt in Mastery mode;
explanatory feedback available in Practice mode; and
approved extra-time accommodations supported in institutional use.

The quiz tests whether you can recognise and apply epidemiological reasoning, not whether you can search for definitions quickly.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 6, you should be able to:

recognise a case-control study from its outcome-based sampling direction;
define cases using criteria independent of the exposure being evaluated;
explain the idea of the source population that gave rise to the cases;
select controls that estimate the exposure distribution expected in that source population;
explain why convenient controls can introduce selection bias;
calculate and interpret an odds ratio from a two-by-two table;
explain why risks and risk ratios are generally not directly estimable from a standard case-control sample;
distinguish odds from probability and avoid interpreting an odds ratio as a risk ratio when inappropriate;
recognise recall, interviewer and exposure-misclassification threats;
explain when matching may be useful and why matched designs require matched analysis;
identify settings in which case-control studies are efficient, including rare outcomes and outbreak investigations.

LEARN 2 · Defining cases
Case selection begins with a reproducible outcome definition.
A strong case definition should make clear:

clinical or diagnostic criteria;
relevant person, place and time restrictions;
whether cases are incident or prevalent;
how diagnostic certainty is handled; and
where cases are being ascertained.

Do not define a case using the suspected exposure that you later intend to test. If “ate beef” is part of the case definition, beef will inevitably appear more common among cases because exposure has been built into outcome classification.

LEARN 8 · Recall and interviewer bias
Case-control studies often reconstruct previous exposure after outcome status is known.
This creates opportunities for information bias.
Cases may search their memories more intensely than controls because they want an explanation for illness. Interviewers who know the hypothesis may probe cases more deeply. Records may be more complete for severely ill cases than controls.
Ways to reduce these problems include:

standardised questionnaires;
objective records or biomarkers where appropriate;
similar recall periods;
interviewer training;
blinding exposure assessors when feasible; and
asking questions in the same manner across groups.

Perfect memory is not required; comparable measurement error is the goal.

LEARN 11 · When case-control designs are especially useful
Case-control studies are particularly attractive when:

the outcome is rare;
the disease has a long latency;
exposure data can be reconstructed efficiently;
the source population is large or difficult to enumerate;
rapid outbreak investigation is needed; or
multiple prior exposures are being evaluated.

They are less attractive when the exposure is rare and difficult to ascertain retrospectively, or when reliable control selection is impossible.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 8, you should be able to:

distinguish experimental from observational epidemiological studies;
explain what random allocation is intended to achieve;
distinguish individual and cluster allocation conceptually;
explain the roles of control groups, allocation concealment and blinding at an introductory level;
distinguish efficacy from effectiveness;
recognise common threats created by non-adherence and loss to follow-up;
explain intention-to-treat conceptually;
distinguish simple before-after evaluation from stronger controlled or interrupted designs;
identify when randomisation is unethical, infeasible or unnecessary;
write causal conclusions in proportion to the strength of the design and execution.

LEARN 3 · The comparison group remains essential
Without a comparison group, a change after intervention may reflect:

natural recovery;
regression to the mean;
secular trends;
seasonal change;
another intervention;
changing measurement or reporting; or
changes in who is observed.

Randomised trials create a contemporaneous comparison intended to represent what would have happened without the intervention.
That counterfactual logic is the real source of causal strength.

LEARN 10 · Design does not compensate for poor execution
A randomised label cannot save:

major loss to follow-up;
differential outcome measurement;
contamination;
protocol changes;
selective reporting;
poor adherence information; or
analysis that ignores the unit of randomisation.

Conversely, a well-designed quasi-experiment can sometimes provide more credible evidence than a badly executed trial.
Judge the actual comparison created, not only the design name.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 10, you should be able to:

construct a two-by-two table comparing an index test with a reference classification;
calculate and interpret sensitivity and specificity;
calculate and interpret positive and negative predictive values;
explain why predictive values change with disease prevalence even when sensitivity and specificity remain constant;
distinguish sensitivity/specificity from predictive values by their conditioning direction;
calculate and interpret likelihood ratios at an introductory level;
explain how changing a test threshold trades sensitivity against specificity;
interpret ROC curves and AUC conceptually without treating AUC as a complete measure of usefulness;
compare serial and parallel testing strategies conceptually;
distinguish diagnostic accuracy from agreement between tests;
recognise verification, spectrum and imperfect-reference-standard problems;
interpret a positive result in clinical and public-health context rather than as certainty of disease.

LEARN 9 · ROC curves and AUC

LIPPS extension ROC analysis is included as an advanced introductory extension.

A receiver operating characteristic curve plots sensitivity against 1 − specificity across thresholds.
The area under the curve (AUC) summarises discrimination: how well the test tends to rank diseased individuals above non-diseased individuals.
AUC does not tell you:

PPV in your population;
whether a chosen threshold is clinically useful;
whether the test is calibrated;
whether harms and costs are acceptable.

A good AUC is not the same as a good screening programme.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 12, you should be able to:

distinguish description, association, prediction and causal effect questions;
identify the estimand implied by a research question and reported measure at an introductory level;
judge whether the study design establishes a credible temporal and counterfactual comparison;
evaluate outcome, exposure and covariate measurement as potential sources of error;
assess selection bias, information bias and confounding as threats to interpretation;
interpret effect size together with confidence intervals rather than p-values alone;
distinguish statistical significance from scientific and public-health importance;
separate internal validity from applicability to another population;
recognise competing explanations and residual uncertainty;
write conclusions using causal language only when the design and assumptions justify it;
recommend action under uncertainty without pretending evidence is complete;
apply the LIPPS EVIDENCE appraisal framework to a paper, report or programme claim.

LEARN 2 · Name the estimand before interpreting the estimate

LIPPS extension The attached CDC course does not use modern estimand terminology explicitly. It is introduced here because it clarifies what an analysis is trying to estimate.

An estimand is the target quantity defined by the scientific question.
Examples:

prevalence in a target population;
risk ratio over 12 months;
risk difference under intervention versus no intervention;
odds ratio for prior exposure in a case-control design;
incidence rate ratio;
effect of assignment to a programme.

An estimate can be mathematically correct while targeting the wrong estimand for the decision question.

LEARN 6 · Bias and confounding are alternative explanations
The attached source defines selection bias, information bias and confounding and emphasises valid comparison groups.
A useful appraisal question is:

What process, other than the proposed causal mechanism, could have generated this estimate?

Possible answers include:

differential enrolment;
differential loss to follow-up;
exposure recall differences;
outcome ascertainment differences;
uncontrolled confounding;
post-exposure adjustment;
unmeasured structural factors; or
data-processing error.

The goal is not to invent endless hypothetical bias. It is to identify plausible mechanisms strong enough to matter.

LEARN 7 · P-values address compatibility with a null model, not importance
The attached CDC outbreak lesson describes the p-value as the probability of observing an association as large or larger if the null hypothesis were true, and cautions that statistical tests are guides rather than guarantees.
A small p-value does not tell you:

the probability that the null hypothesis is true;
the probability the alternative hypothesis is true;
whether the result is clinically important;
whether bias is absent;
whether the study is well designed; or
whether the effect will generalise.

Likewise, p &gt; 0.05 does not prove “no effect,” especially when the study is imprecise.

LEARN 12 · Evidence can support action without proving everything
The attached CDC course makes an important practical point: epidemiology may not prove a causal relationship with certainty, yet it can provide enough evidence for appropriate prevention and control.
Public-health decisions often balance:

strength and coherence of evidence;
severity and urgency of the problem;
plausibility of benefit;
potential harms;
reversibility of the intervention;
cost and feasibility; and
consequences of waiting.

The correct communication is not false certainty. It is a transparent statement of what is known, uncertain and reasonable to do next.

Learning outcomes
By the end of Week 3, you should be able to:

explain why comparison is central to analytic epidemiology;
organise binary exposure and outcome data in a two-by-two table;
calculate and interpret risk ratios;
calculate and interpret rate ratios;
calculate and interpret risk differences / excess risk;
distinguish odds from risk and calculate an odds ratio;
explain why an odds ratio can approximate a risk ratio when an outcome is uncommon and why the two can diverge when the outcome is common;
identify when an odds ratio is the natural measure in a case-control study;
calculate and interpret a prevalence ratio in cross-sectional data;
distinguish relative from absolute measures of association;
calculate and interpret an attributable proportion among the exposed under appropriate causal assumptions;
calculate and interpret vaccine efficacy/effectiveness from risks or a risk ratio; and
interpret measures of association without making causal claims that the design and evidence cannot support.

